    intro: `Web development is the practice of building the systems, structures, and interactions that make websites and web applications function. It is a discipline that sits at the intersection of logic and communication — you must think in precise, systematic terms about how information flows and how machines execute instructions, while simultaneously keeping in mind how real human beings will experience the result. Most people who begin learning web development focus on syntax: they learn what tags to use, which CSS properties exist, and how JavaScript functions are written. That focus is understandable, but it produces brittle knowledge that breaks the moment circumstances change. The professional foundation of web development is not syntax — it is understanding. When you understand why the web works the way it does, you can navigate unfamiliar tools, recover from errors, and make decisions that hold up over time. This guide builds that foundational understanding from the ground up, covering the mental models and principles that underpin everything else in web development.`,
